# Lists.
#
# Three primitives, and every consumer above them written by index
# recursion: `listAt` gives an `Option of T` and `listLength` gives a
# bound, so a fold over a list is an ordinary recursive function.
#
# `listLength` and `listAt` are primitives because shrinking a collection
# needs a tail, and a tail is a collection — which the language cannot
# construct at a length not written in the source. `reverse` is a primitive
# for the same reason. That is the whole of the list layer's dependence on
# the platform.
#
# ⚠️ THE COST, STATED. There are no local bindings in ZDeceptron, so a
# fold cannot carry an accumulator through a loop and every one of these
# recurses once per element. Stack depth is therefore linear in the length
# of the collection, and a long enough list runs the host out of stack
# rather than returning. `crates/zdc-codegen/tests/depth.rs` pins that
# behaviour so it is a recorded limit rather than a surprise.
#
# This is the finding §14F.2 asked for — a fact about the language, not a
# reason to reach for the FFI — and §17.4.10 already names the fix and
# calls it the single change with the largest return: local bindings, plus
# a `rest of`, turn every function below into a loop and move `split`,
# `reverse`, `keys` and `values` into ZDeceptron as well.

foreign listLength is anywhere
    from "zd:list" as "length"
    takes of value is List of T
    gives Whole

foreign listAt is anywhere
    from "zd:list" as "at"
    takes value is List of T, index is Whole
    gives Option of T

foreign reverse is anywhere
    from "zd:list" as "reverse"
    takes of value is List of T
    gives List of T

function first of items
    give listAt with value is items, index is 0

function last of items
    give listAt with value is items, index is (listLength of items) - 1

function isEmpty of items
    give (listLength of items) is 0

# The target of `items contains value` when `items` is a List.
function listContains with items, value
    give listContainsFrom with items is items, value is value, index is 0

function listContainsFrom with items, value, index
    when listAt with value is items, index is index
        None
            give no
        Some with item
            if item is value
                give yes
            give listContainsFrom with items is items, value is value, index is index + 1

function sumOf of numbers
    give sumFrom with numbers is numbers, index is 0

function sumFrom with numbers, index
    when listAt with value is numbers, index is index
        None
            give 0
        Some with value
            give value + (sumFrom with numbers is numbers, index is index + 1)

function join with parts, using
    give joinFrom with parts is parts, using is using, index is 0

function joinFrom with parts, using, index
    when listAt with value is parts, index is index
        None
            give ""
        Some with piece
            if index + 1 < (listLength of parts)
                give piece + using + (joinFrom with parts is parts, using is using, index is index + 1)
            give piece
